# Global banner

Use a global banner for an important message that affects the whole interface and is not an emergency. Say what is affected and what to do next, and define what dismissing it means.

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation. They do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## Use the shared notice decision

Start with the [message decision guide](../../components/status.html#choose-the-right-message) and the [ordinary banner contract](../../components/notification-banners.html#child-civica-notification-banner-pattern). This variant adds whole-service relevance and a dismissal lifecycle. It does not give permission to create a global native Home Assistant notice, and it does not make a routine message urgent.

## When to use it

Use it for a bounded interruption or a change that affects every page, such as planned maintenance on the documentation site. A problem with one room’s device belongs with that device. A release announcement usually belongs in update information, not on every dashboard.

## Structure and behaviour

Keep a short heading, one concise paragraph and at most one main destination. If the message can be dismissed, give the action a visible label and say whether dismissal applies per user, per browser or per message revision. An important operational fault must stay discoverable after dismissal.

## Home Assistant boundary

The official guide describes a limited display-count cookie for some government campaigns. Civica does not adopt that. This reference adds no cookie, cross-site tracking or global Home Assistant message. Persistence needs an explicit implementation and a documented lifecycle.

